WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.020
Why can't a leaf take carbon dioxide from the air without losing water?

2
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.380
Taking something in often carries a cost somewhere else.

3
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.540
A leaf makes that cost easy to see.

4
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.050
Every multicellular organism faces the same five problems.

5
00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:23.730
Get energy and materials.

6
00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:27.170
Exchange and move them, wastes included.

7
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.490
Keep its inside relatively stable.

8
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.250
Resist damage and invasion.

9
00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:35.690
Leave descendants.

10
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:40.273
Barley, honeybee or human, each body solves all five.

11
00:00:41.593 --> 00:00:44.473
Many solutions carry a cost, paid somewhere else.

12
00:00:45.693 --> 00:00:48.413
The exchange problem shows that cost most clearly.

13
00:00:49.517 --> 00:00:51.197
Zoom into a barley leaf.

14
00:00:52.397 --> 00:00:56.237
A waxy layer covers the surface, and water crosses it poorly.

15
00:00:57.637 --> 00:01:00.097
So gas moves mainly through pores called stomata.

16
00:01:01.377 --> 00:01:04.877
Two guard cells flank each pore and open or close the gap.

17
00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:11.590
Inside the leaf, cell walls line air spaces, and those walls are wet.

18
00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:17.190
Carbon dioxide must dissolve in that water film to reach the cells.

19
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.830
But a wet surface open to air loses water.

20
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:26.850
So water vapour leaves through the same pore that lets carbon dioxide in.

21
00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.750
Both gases take one path.

22
00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.450
What, then, sets the price, the water lost for each carbon gained?

23
00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:37.483
Look at two gaps across the pore.

24
00:01:38.803 --> 00:01:40.603
First, the carbon dioxide gap.

25
00:01:41.943 --> 00:01:46.783
Carbon dioxide is a tiny share of air, and the leaf draws it down only part of the

26
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:47.783
way.

27
00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:49.423
Now the water gap.

28
00:01:50.723 --> 00:01:55.623
Air inside a leaf is saturated with water vapour, while outside air is drier.

29
00:01:56.723 --> 00:01:58.003
That gap is far bigger.

30
00:01:59.283 --> 00:02:03.463
So the leaf pays hundreds of water molecules for each carbon dioxide.

31
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:08.587
Now let the guard cells narrow the pore, with both gaps held fixed.

32
00:02:09.927 --> 00:02:11.307
Both streams shrink together.

33
00:02:12.507 --> 00:02:14.427
The pores set how fast gas moves.

34
00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:16.947
The two gaps set the price.

35
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:21.137
Your lung also moves a gas across a wet surface.

36
00:02:22.457 --> 00:02:23.517
It takes in oxygen.

37
00:02:24.937 --> 00:02:27.897
Will it pay more water for each molecule of gas, or less?

38
00:02:34.433 --> 00:02:40.973
Far less. Oxygen is plentiful in air, so each breath can hand over a lot of it.

39
00:02:42.153 --> 00:02:45.013
The breath out carries away about as much water vapour.

40
00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:49.773
So the lung pays about one water molecule for each oxygen.

41
00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:53.373
But the lung cannot close the way a stoma can.

42
00:02:54.533 --> 00:02:56.333
It sits sheltered deep in the chest.

43
00:02:57.593 --> 00:03:03.073
In ordinary unsaturated air, narrowing or sheltering a path can cut the water cost.

44
00:03:04.253 --> 00:03:07.453
But while gas keeps flowing in, some water still leaves.

45
00:03:08.533 --> 00:03:09.733
Back to the checklist.

46
00:03:11.073 --> 00:03:15.053
For every row, ask what a solution costs, and where that cost is paid.

47
00:03:16.373 --> 00:03:17.853
Then ask how you would test it.
